<p>The till is a full-screen page made for a barcode scanner and a busy counter. Open it from <strong>Open till</strong> in the top bar, or
  <strong>Start selling</strong> on a cashier's Home. Each computer remembers which till it is. The list of tills says whose shift is
  open on each; every device on a till adds to the same sale and the same cash drawer, so HardPoint asks before you join someone
  else's.</p>

<%= render "help/walkthrough", tour: HelpTour.find("till") %>

<h2>Opening the shift</h2>
<p>Count the cash in the drawer and press <strong>Open shift</strong> with that float. Everything taken until you close is counted against it.</p>

<h2>Ringing up a sale</h2>
<ol>
  <li>Scan barcodes, type part of a name or SKU and pick the product, or tap through the categories. Quick-pick buttons cover fast movers and pack sizes.</li>
  <li>Change the quantity for loose goods: 2.5 metres, 0.75 kg. A box's barcode adds the box at its pack price.</li>
  <li>Choose a customer if the sale goes on their account, needs their KRA PIN on the receipt, or is for one of their
    jobs. Their price list applies as soon as you pick them. Choose them before taking any money: once a payment is on the sale,
    the customer can't be changed until it's removed.</li>
  <li>If you're taking the goods to site, <strong>Add transport</strong> puts the delivery charge on the sale, with where it's going.</li>
  <li>Promotions apply by themselves. To give a discount, use the line's or the sale's discount, as an amount or a percentage.</li>
  <li>Press <strong>Take payment</strong>: cash (the till works out the change), M-Pesa, Airtel Money, card, foreign notes, on account for
    approved customers, or a mix. If the sale comes to nothing (a replacement under warranty discounted to zero, say), there's no payment
    to take: press <strong>Complete sale, nothing to pay</strong> instead. A cashier needs a manager's approval PIN for that.</li>
  <li>The receipt prints. KRA's signature and QR code are on it when eTIMS is connected. It can also be emailed or texted, or sent with <strong>WhatsApp receipt</strong>.</li>
</ol>
<p>Keyboard shortcuts: <strong>F2</strong> goes to the scan box, <strong>F8</strong> parks the sale, <strong>F9</strong> takes payment.</p>

<h2>On a touch screen</h2>
<p>The till works without a keyboard or scanner, on a touch-screen POS terminal, a tablet or a phone:</p>
<ul>
  <li>Tap <strong>Quick picks</strong> or a category along the top of the products, then tap a product. A box asks how many, with 1 ready: tap <strong>Add to sale</strong> (or press Enter), or type
    the quantity or use <strong>+</strong> and <strong>−</strong> first. Scanning a barcode still adds one straight away. A category shows its
    subcategories' products too. Products are put into categories on the product form.</li>
  <li>Use <strong>+</strong> and <strong>−</strong> on a line to change the quantity, or tap the number to type one, and <strong>✕</strong>
    to take it off.</li>
  <li>Tap <strong>Cash received</strong> (or the amount for M-Pesa or card) and a number pad opens. <strong>Exact</strong> fills in the
    amount due, <strong>C</strong> clears it and <strong>⌫</strong> takes off a digit. The button with the dots beside the amount opens
    and closes the pad, and each till remembers how you left it.</li>
  <li>The device's own keyboard stays out of the way while a scanner is plugged in; tap the scan box when you want to type a search.</li>
  <li>On a phone, the till shows the products and the sale one at a time: switch with <strong>Products</strong> and <strong>Sale</strong> at the
    top. The Sale tab shows how many items are in the sale and its total.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Sending documents by WhatsApp</h2>
<p>Receipts, quotes and orders, statements, purchase orders and delivery notes have a <strong>WhatsApp</strong> button. It opens WhatsApp with
  a short message and a link to the document, addressed to the customer's or supplier's phone number. The link works for 30 days and shows
  only that one document. See <%= link_to "Sending documents on WhatsApp", help_article_path("whatsapp"), class: "link" %>.</p>

<h2>Parking a sale</h2>
<p>If a customer goes to fetch something, <strong>Park this sale</strong> and serve the next person. Parked sales wait on the till until you
  <strong>Recall</strong> them. <strong>Clear sale</strong> throws away a cart you don't want.</p>

<h2>Things that need a manager</h2>
<p>A discount above the shop's limit, a void, a return, or a sale that takes a customer past their credit limit asks for an owner's or
  manager's approval PIN. They type it at the till; it approves that one thing and doesn't sign them in. An approved discount covers
  that discount only: a bigger one later on the same sale, on another line or on the whole sale, asks again. See
  <%= link_to "Staff, roles and PINs", help_article_path("staff-and-roles"), class: "link" %>.</p>

<h2>Returns and voids</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Return</strong>: find the sale under <strong>Sales</strong> and press <strong>Return items</strong> to refund part or all of it,
    putting the goods back into stock or writing them off, and refunding by cash, M-Pesa, Airtel Money, card or account credit.</li>
  <li><strong>Void</strong>: <strong>Void sale</strong> cancels a sale made in error, as long as its shift is still open. After the shift has
    closed, use a return instead.</li>
</ul>
<p>More in <%= link_to "Returns, refunds and voids", help_article_path("returns-and-refunds"), class: "link" %>.</p>

<h2>Cash during the day</h2>
<p><strong>Cash drop / payout</strong> records cash taken to the safe, paid out (for transport, say) or paid in. The <strong>X report</strong>
  shows how the shift is doing without closing it.</p>

<h2>Closing the shift</h2>
<p>At the end of the day, press <strong>Close shift</strong> and count the drawer. The count is blind: the till doesn't show what it expects
  until you've entered what's there. HardPoint then shows the difference and prints the <strong>Z report</strong>. Past shifts are under
  <strong>Sell › Shifts</strong>. More in <%= link_to "Shifts, cash and closing up", help_article_path("shifts-and-cash"), class: "link" %>.</p>
